    "sources/RTAnalyticSource": {
      "short": "Create a rectilinear grid containing a wavelet",
      "long": "Create a regular rectilinear grid in up to three dimensions with values varying according to a periodic function.",
      "description": "The Wavelet source can be used to create a uniform rectilinear grid in up to three dimensions with values varying according to the following periodic function. OS = M * exp(-r^2 / (2 * SD^2)) + XM * sin(XF * x) + YM * sin(YF * y) + ZM * cos(ZF * z). OS is the output scalar; M is the maximum value (the Gaussian amplitude) and SD the standard deviation of the Gaussian; r is the distance from the centre; XM, YM, and ZM are the X, Y, and Z magnitude values; and XF, YF, and ZF are the X, Y, and Z frequency values. The terms are added, not multiplied, and the coordinates are offsets from the centre divided by the extent width (vtkRTAnalyticSource; ParaView's own documentation writes the terms as a product, Schemaxis correction). If a two-dimensional extent is specified, the resulting image will be displayed. If a three-dimensional extent is used, then the bounding box of the volume will be displayed.",
      "selection": "Create a rectilinear grid containing a wavelet",
      "basis": "curated correction of installed native documentation",
      "captured": {
        "description": "The Wavelet source can be used to create a uniform rectilinear grid in up to three dimensions with values varying according to the following periodic function. OS = M * G * (XM * sin(XF * x) + YM * sin(YF * y) + ZM * cos(ZF * z)) OS is the output scalar; M represents the maximum value; G represents the Gaussian; XM, YM, and ZM are the X, Y, and Z magnitude values; and XF, YF, and ZF are the X, Y, and Z frequency values. If a two-dimensional extent is specified, the resulting image will be displayed. If a three-dimensional extent is used, then the bounding box of the volume will be displayed."
      },
      "properties": {
